<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomponius Mela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 43 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pomponius Mela was a Roman geographer from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> author of a surviving geographical work. His De Chorographia, written around 43 CE, describes the coasts of the known world, with notes on peoples and places. It is an important source for peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, such as the Ausci and Sordones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela.jpg" alt="Pomponius Mela" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Américo Toledano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_de_Pomponius_Mela_-_Ceuta_001_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
